' THE CASINO
' One evening, one wallet, six tables, for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu. Each table is a game already in this toolbox, its rules and
' pictures kept, sharing the money you walk in with:
' - CASINO BLACKJACK by John Hamilton, COMPUTE! magazine, March 1988
'   (Commodore 64; Atari and Apple II versions by William Chin, IBM PC and
'   Amiga by George Miller): Las Vegas rules, blackjack pays 3 to 2,
'   insurance, double down on 10 or 11, split, surrender (games/bj).
' - ROULETTE, LAS VEGAS HIGH, SEVEN/ELEVEN, UNDER 'N' OVER and INNER
'   SPRING from Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games (Ballantine
'   Books, 1984; Interface Publications, 1983): the American wheel and the
'   book's sixteen bets and odds (games/rl), the fruit machine and its
'   payouts (games/lv), two dice games (games/dg) and the card game of
'   games/cp.
'
' You walk in with $100. The lobby shows your wallet; pick a table with
' the stick and the button. At every table the stick sets your bet (up
' and down by $1, left and right by $10) and the button plays; the second
' button takes you back to the lobby. Lose it all and the house sees you
' to the door kindly, with a fresh $100 for your next visit. Keypad not
' used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - One wallet for every table (the games each gave their own stake: $100,
'   1,000 chips, $250, $105, $30, $20), up to $65,000; bets from $1 to the
'   table limit ($1,000; $500 at the wheel).
' - SEVEN/ELEVEN is played for a bet you choose, paid 1 to 1 (the book
'   charged $5 a round and paid $20, so it could not be lost).
' - Blackjack takes any whole-dollar bet; a 3 to 2 payout or a half bet
'   (insurance, surrender) drops an odd half dollar in the house's favour.
' - The book's ends of the evening ("YOU HAVE BROKEN THE BANK" at 2,000
'   chips, the fruit machine's $2,500 limit) are left out: the evening ends
'   when you leave.
' - The fruit machine's bet uses the casino's stick (up/down $1,
'   left/right $10); the button pulls the handle.
' - Each table's rules, pictures, sounds and words come from its own
'   cartridge (see those games' headers for their own liberties); the
'   lobby, the shared bet line, the bursts of sparks on a big win and the
'   music (Lucky Lights) are new.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; at most four
' sprites on a line (the eight sparks fly apart on different lines); the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI and every screen is drawn with the
' NMI off after a wait.

